<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=manhattan-project">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Weapons and war</p><h1 class="enc-title">Manhattan Project</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1942">1942</a>–<a href="/years/1946">1946</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manhattan">Manhattan</a> Project was the programme led by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, with British and Canadian participation, that developed the first atomic bombs during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>. Directed by General Leslie Groves, with J. Robert Oppenheimer heading the Los Alamos laboratory, it produced the weapons used on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiroshima">Hiroshima</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagasaki">Nagasaki</a> in August 1945. Much of its uranium came from the Belgian Congo.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/manhattan-project.jpg" alt="Manhattan Project" width="320" height="174" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author or not provided · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Time,_3-22_p.m,_December_2,_1942._Place,_Racquets_Court_under_West_Stands_of_Stagg_Field,_University_of_Chicago...._-_NARA_-_542144.tif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The discovery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuclear-fission">nuclear fission</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-hahn">Otto Hahn</a> and Fritz Strassmann in 1938, and its theoretical explanation by Lise Meitner and Otto Frisch, made a uranium bomb theoretically possible, and there were fears that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> would build one first. In August 1939 Leo Szilard and Eugene Wigner drafted a letter, signed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-einstein">Albert Einstein</a> and delivered to President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franklin-d-roosevelt">Franklin D. Roosevelt</a>, that warned of &quot;extremely powerful bombs of a new type&quot;.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, Otto Robert Frisch and Rudolf Peierls calculated in 1939 that the critical mass of uranium-235 was within an order of magnitude of 10 kilograms, and their March 1940 memorandum began the British project, Tube Alloys. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> committee meeting of 18 December 1941 met just after the attack on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attack-on-pearl-harbor">Pearl Harbor</a>, and work went ahead on several methods of isotope separation and on reactors.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In June 1942 Colonel James C. Marshall began to head the Army's part from a temporary headquarters at 270 Broadway in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manhattan">Manhattan</a>, which gave the Manhattan District its name. Major General Leslie Groves then took over the project, which employed nearly 130,000 people at its peak and cost nearly 2 billion dollars. Enriched uranium was produced at the Clinton Engineer Works at Oak Ridge in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tennessee">Tennessee</a>, on land acquired by eminent domain that affected about 1,000 families. Plutonium came from the world's first industrial-scale reactors at Hanford in Washington. J. Robert Oppenheimer directed the Los Alamos Laboratory in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-mexico">New Mexico</a>, which designed two weapons: the uranium gun-type Little Boy and the plutonium implosion bomb Fat Man.</p><p>The key raw material was uranium. In 1940 there were four known major deposits, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colorado">Colorado</a>, northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jachymov">Joachimsthal</a> and the Belgian Congo, and Kenneth Nichols negotiated the purchase of 1,200 short tons of ore from the Congo stored on Staten Island.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> supplied uranium and heavy water. The Fat Man design was at first a low-priority fallback, but in 1944 it was confirmed that Hanford plutonium was unsuitable for a gun-type bomb. The first nuclear device was detonated at the Trinity test in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-mexico">New Mexico</a> on 16 July 1945.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The project delivered the bombs used in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atomic-bombings-of-hiroshima-and-nagasaki">atomic bombings</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiroshima">Hiroshima</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagasaki">Nagasaki</a> in August 1945. Through Operation Alsos its personnel also gathered intelligence on the German nuclear programme, and Soviet spies penetrated the project despite its emphasis on secrecy. After the war it tested weapons at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bikini-atoll">Bikini Atoll</a> in Operation Crossroads, helped to create the network of national laboratories and laid the foundations of the nuclear navy. It kept control of American atomic weapons research and production until the United States Atomic Energy Commission was formed in January 1947.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Manhattan Project” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Roosevelt set up an advisory committee that led eventually to the <b>Manhattan Project</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-11">11 October in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Union Minière du Haut Katanga mined copper and cobalt, and uranium from Shinkolobwe supplied the <b>Manhattan Project</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/belgian-congo">Belgian Congo</a></li></ul></section>
